    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `Optimiser.hill_climb`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `hill_climb` function is an optimization method used to refine a test case by iteratively modifying its blocks to improve its quality. It starts from the end of the current test case data and examines each block, attempting to replace its content with different integer values to see if the resulting test case is better according to predefined criteria. The function uses a helper function `attempt_replace` to try replacing a block with a new integer value and checks if the new test case is valid and better than the current one. It scans both upwards (increasing the integer value) and downwards (decreasing the integer value) to explore the space of possible replacements. The function relies on the `engine.cached_test_function` to evaluate the quality of new test cases and the `consider_new_data` method to decide if a new test case should replace the current one. It handles edge cases by skipping blocks that have already been examined and by stopping if the maximum number of improvements is reached. The function does not return any value; instead, it modifies the `current_data` attribute of the `Optimiser` instance in place.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
